                <pb facs="0379" n="261" sameAs="frus:frus1928v02/pg261"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">393.1163/255: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in China</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">MacMurray</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peking</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1928-04-25"><hi rendition="#italic">April 25, 1928</hi>—(?) <hi
                                rendition="#italic">p.m.</hi></date><lb/>[Received April 25—11
                        p.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>271. A report dated April 16th from Vice Consul Paxton quotes from letters from a
                    considerable number of missionaries in Kiangsu and Anhwei indicating that, in
                    general, missionaries who have returned to their stations have found diminution
                    in antiforeign attitude on the part of both civilians and troops but that in
                    spite of this and assurances given by Nanking government the greater part of
                    American buildings are still occupied by the military.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">MacMurray</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
